 \begin{document}
 \maketitle
 \begin{abstract}
-We prove an attained envelope for mixed centered permutation products, allowing arbitrary multiplicities and independent colors. When no conflicting centered singleton occurs, compatible cells in one permutation attain the exact maximum for fixed support and singleton counts. Scalar consequences sharpen the probabilistic estimate used in random-lift and biregular spectral-gap arguments. For distinct diagonal cells, the leading terms of Collins and Nagatsu are relatively accurate exactly when $k^3/N\to0$, answering their published optimal-scale question. The critical limits are parity-dependent hyperbolic functions. A positive partition expansion and an elementary saddle estimate give a uniform approximation throughout $k=o(N)$, a sharp hierarchy of correction thresholds, and an exact-denominator formula for every $2\le k\le N$. On bounded square-root ranges we determine the signed first error and the sharp constant in its uniform $N^{-1/4}$ rate. We explain the spectral application and isolate two failures in an intermediate conditioning formula without asserting a new graph eigenvalue theorem.
+We prove attained envelopes for mixed centered permutation products, allowing arbitrary multiplicities and independent colors. When at most one centered singleton conflicts with another requested cell, explicit patterns in one permutation attain the exact maximum for fixed support and singleton counts. A stronger bound also applies when several centered singletons conflict. Scalar consequences sharpen the probabilistic estimate used in random-lift and biregular spectral-gap arguments. For distinct diagonal cells, the leading terms of Collins and Nagatsu are relatively accurate exactly when $k^3/N\to0$, answering their published optimal-scale question. The critical limits are parity-dependent hyperbolic functions. A positive partition expansion and an elementary saddle estimate give a uniform approximation throughout $k=o(N)$, a sharp hierarchy of correction thresholds, and an exact-denominator formula for every $2\le k\le N$. For the latter formula we determine the full parity error profile and the optimal uniform rate $C_*N^{-1/3}+o(N^{-1/3})$, with $C_*\approx0.39549627$. On bounded square-root ranges we determine the signed first error and the sharp constant in its uniform $N^{-1/4}$ rate. We explain the spectral application and isolate two failures in an intermediate conditioning formula without asserting a new graph eigenvalue theorem.
 \end{abstract}
